# transfer.bot -- the "std::transfer" namespace: copying a sendable value
# from one Botlish VM to another (SENDABILITY-AND-VALUE-COPY-MILESTONE-1.md).
#
#   data = std::transfer::encode(value)          # std::abi::bytes::Bytes
#   copy = std::transfer::decode[T](data)        # a T of this VM's own
#
# A value is *sendable* (the intrinsic trait Sendable) when an equivalent
# value can be reconstructed in another VM without anything of the sending
# VM: no heap object, no runtime resource (a coroutine, a C pointer, a
# mailbox or event registration), no execution environment and no
# authority (an installed context). The compiler derives it from the
# value's static type: scalars, Strings, Lists, ImmutableSets,
# MutableVectors and MutableArrays of sendable elements, structs (and trees)
# of sendable fields, enum cases; an opaque struct only when its owning
# module states how a copy is rebuilt (its `sendable` clause: the standard
# library's byte storage and collections do). A call of either operation on
# a value whose static type is not sendable is NOT-SENDABLE, with the path
# to the component that is not.
#
# encode writes the value into owned bytes: no address, no number of this
# compilation's making (a struct shape, an enum number), and no reference
# into this VM; the value is unchanged. decode reads bytes made by a
# program compiled with the same declarations of the value's type -- the
# same program in another VM, or another program -- into a value allocated
# entirely in this VM: Strings, byte storage and collections are made anew,
# a map's or set's derived state (its index, its stored hashes) is computed
# again by this VM, and nothing of the bytes or the sender is kept. A
# mutable collection received is a new value: mutating it changes no other
# copy, here or in the sender.
#
# decode never trusts the bytes. It refuses, with a declared error and
# nothing changed:
#
#   TransferMalformed      bytes that are no transfer (no header, cut short,
#                          a count larger than the bytes left, bytes after
#                          the value)
#   TransferVersion        a format version this runtime does not read
#   TransferTypeMismatch   a value of another type (its schema's fingerprint
#                          is not T's)
#   TransferInvalidValue   a value outside its type: a Bool byte other than
#                          0 or 1, text that is not UTF-8, a non-finite
#                          Float64, an Int outside its integer domain, a
#                          case index of no case, a List of another stated
#                          length
#   TransferLimit          a decode beyond the runtime's limits (an Int of
#                          more than 2^20 bytes, more than 2^24 elements of
#                          no size)
#
# The bytes are a transfer between running programs, not a storage format:
# their version is the compiler's, and nothing promises a later compiler
# reads them.
#
# Not here, deliberately: threads, actor messages across VMs (std::actor's
# messages stay ordinary values of one VM), sharing a heap, moving an affine
# value (a coroutine, a C pointer) to another VM, and any authority: a
# decoded value is data, and the receiver's contexts are its own.
import std::abi::bytes
